  title="Vela Sea · VelaWind"
  type="article"
  description="A maritime simulator whose simulation layer runs, and is tested, with no window open."
>
  <main class="page" id="main">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/">VelaWind</a> · <a href="/projects">Projects</a></p>

    <h1>Vela Sea</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A maritime simulator whose simulation layer runs, and is tested, with no
      window open.
      <br />
      <a href={downloadUrl}>Windows download</a> · <a href={liveUrl}>Web demo</a> · <a href={repoUrl}>Source</a>
    </p>

    <CaseShot href="/projects/vela-sea" />

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A game loop and a simulation want to live in the same objects. The thing
      that knows a vessel's heading is the thing that knows how to draw it, and
      the frame that advances the world is the frame that paints it. That is the
      path of least resistance, and it is also the end of being able to check
      anything. Once the two are one thing, nothing can be verified without
      opening a window, so confirming that a vessel handles a crossing correctly
      means playing it and watching.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the obvious approach did not work</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious fix is discipline: keep drawing out of the simulation and be
      careful. That does not hold, because "be careful" has no failure mode.
      Nothing reports it broken. No build turns red on the day a rectangle from
      the drawing layer ends up inside the physics, and the drift only becomes
      visible much later, when the test that was supposed to exist turns out to
      be impossible to write.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the rule is stated instead as a property a machine can check: nothing
      under <code>engine/</code>, <code>data/</code> or <code>config.py</code>
      imports Pygame. That is one grep, and it either passes or it does not. That
      single constraint is what lets a 16-scenario bot play the whole game under
      SDL's dummy video and audio drivers, with no display and no sound card,
      through movement, contracts, zone fines, grounding damage, hull failure,
      fuel exhaustion and rescue dispatch.
    </p>
    <p>
      The place this is most visible is the one that looks like a workaround.
      In <code>engine/collision.py</code> at line 164 the router imports its
      waypoint list from <code>data/world_data.py</code> inside the function that
      needs it rather than at the top of the file, so that
      loading <code>engine/</code> does not pull in <code>data/</code>. That is
      the rule being obeyed at the point where obeying it costs something, not
      waived because it had become inconvenient. A module-level import would have
      been tidier to read and would have quietly made the layer depend on the
      thing it is not allowed to depend on.
    </p>

    <h2>One trade-off</h2>
    <p>
      Collision avoidance does not run inside the fixed timestep. It runs once
      per rendered frame, at <code>main.py</code> lines 2357 to 2368. Inside the
      simulation loop it ran up to 375 times per frame, and that O(n²) pair scan
      repeated hundreds of times per frame was the crash at 3× speed, so it was
      lifted out of the loop.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cost is worth stating precisely, because it is not the kind that
      arrives as a bug report. The fixed timestep exists so that the simulation
      does not depend on frame rate. This is the one subsystem exempt from it.
      Avoidance is now sampled at display rate, so the world advances further
      between two give-way decisions on a slow machine than on a fast one, which
      means a slow machine gets coarser collision behaviour than a fast one. The
      web build halves it again, to every other frame. Nothing in the repository
      measures the difference between them.
    </p>

    <h2>What should change</h2>
    <p>
      The least comfortable one has been fixed, and fixing it found something
      worse. Three checks tested hand-copied code instead of the game's own:
      the course-over-ground check held a copy of a chart function so it could
      run without Pygame, and two avoidance tests re-implemented the steering
      step and the grounding rule from <code>main.py</code>. That is the same
      parallel-texts-drift failure the Lodestar case study is built on,
      reproduced under the same handle, after the argument had already been
      made. The steering step, the grounding rule and the course-over-ground
      maths now live once, in <code>engine/navigation.py</code>, and the game,
      the tests and the verification script call the same functions. A seeded
      6,000-frame run gives identical vessel state and identical frames before
      and after the move.
    </p>
    <p>
      Moving the code showed that the copied chart function was never called by
      the game, so that check had been testing a copy of dead code, and the
      course-over-ground vector it exists to draw was never on the chart. It is
      drawn now, for the player and the followed vessel, whenever drift
      exceeds half a degree.
    </p>
    <p>
      The structural problems were fixed next. <code>main.py</code> held the
      search-and-rescue dispatch and the AI decision helpers: the layering rule
      held by its letter, because <code>main.py</code> was never in its scope,
      but that logic is simulation and sat on the wrong side of the line the
      rule was drawn to protect. It now lives in <code>engine/sar.py</code>
      and <code>engine/ai.py</code>, with the engine reporting event types and
      <code>main.py</code> mapping them to colours, and <code>main.py</code> fell
      from 3,570 to 3,159 lines. The course-over-ground arrow and
      <code>Vessel.move()</code> now share one velocity function. A seeded
      6,000-frame run is bit-identical before and after both changes, in vessel
      state, every rendered frame and the event log, and changing one constant
      by a billionth makes it differ from the first frame, so the check is not
      blind. Vessel spawning and most of the per-step loop are still
      in <code>main.py</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Writing a test for the chart found an accuracy bug a sailor would spot:
      the scale bar and range rings passed nautical miles to a function that
      expects world units, so the bar labelled 50 nautical miles covered 7.5.
      They now show true distances at every zoom, and the test fails on the old
      code.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two others are fixed. <code>engine/rules.py</code> used to be a rules
      engine nothing instantiated, while the speed caps and fines sat inline in
      the game loop; the zone rules now live in it, with only their side
      effects (sound, log, money) left in <code>main.py</code>, and a seeded run
      produces identical fines and caps before and after. And a rescue used to
      count as complete when the rescuer reached the first waypoint of its route,
      so 145 of 543 rescues in a 14-day diagnostic completed with the rescuer
      more than 3.9 nautical miles from the casualty, one of them 58.6 miles
      away. Completion now requires the rescuer to be in range. The cost is
      visible in the same diagnostic: rescuers sail their whole route, so port
      arrivals fall from 686 to 358. The busier sea was partly the bug.
    </p>

    <p class="provenance">
      The rest of this page is fetched from
      the <a href={sourceUrl}>Vela Sea README</a> at build time and rendered here,
      so it is maintained in the repository and not in this site. It covers the
      architecture, the layering rule and how it is enforced, and, under "Known
      limits", what does not work.
    </p>

    <div class="readme" set:html={html} />
  </main>
</Base>
